</ul><h1>Sweet, sweet technological dependence</h1><p>There was a time when your phone could die and basically nothing happened.</p>
<p>You couldn’t make a phone call, obviously.</p>
<p>Except, actually, you could.</p>
<p>Because if you had an old-school landline, the phone company supplied the power through the line, so you could make a call even when the electricity was out.</p>
<p>Which, now that we think about it, is actually an even better example.</p>
<p>Your phone used to be one thing. A phone. If its battery died, you temporarily lost access to that one thing.</p>
<p>We have since voluntarily engineered a civilization in which a dead phone can strand you despite being surrounded by working cars, money, buildings, transportation, and other human beings.</p>
<p>You used to get a taxi by standing on a curb and flapping your arms.</p>
<p>You used to pay for things by handing someone a credit card.</p>
<p>Your boarding pass was a piece of paper.</p>
<p>Your concert ticket was a piece of paper.</p>
<p>Your hotel key was a little plastic card.</p>
<p>Your car key was a key.</p>
<p>Your house key was…also a key.</p>
<p>If you wanted to park somewhere, you put some coins in a meter. You would <em>not</em> discover that this particular six-block section of downtown requires the ParkSmarterNow app, download it, create an account, verify your email address, enter your license plate number, add a payment method, consider push notifications, and then discover that your phone has 2% battery left.</p>
<p><em>Ahem.</em> Sorry. That one hit a little close to home. Anyway…</p>
<p>…directions came from a map.</p>
<p>Menus came from menus.</p>
<p>Proof of insurance lived in the glove compartment.</p>
<p>And if somebody needed to verify that you were really you, they generally found a way to do it without sending a six-digit code to the very device you were trying to use.</p>
<p>This is progress.</p>
<p>And mostly it actually is! We’re not going to sit here pretending we want to go back to navigating with a AAA TripTik and carrying seventeen pieces of laminated plastic everywhere we go. Your phone can summon a car, pay for dinner, navigate across the country, unlock things, buy things, identify you, get you on an airplane, and tell you exactly how many minutes until the Thai food arrives.</p>
<p>Which is amazing.</p>
<p>Right up until the battery dies.</p>
<p>At which point an impressive chunk of modern civilization simply stops recognizing you as a participant.</p>
